A SQL character literal left open across SSL concatenation
(diag.sql_literal_splice, info tier): reported once per call on the
opening piece, beside sql_injection on the same call (deliberate double
emission). The second call opens the literal inside an IN list whose
closing piece also carries a balanced 'Z' literal.
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE Lookup;
	:PARAMETERS sName, sCode;
	:DECLARE aRows;
	aRows := SQLExecute("SELECT custid FROM customers WHERE custname = '" + sName + "'");
	aRows := SQLExecute("SELECT id FROM c WHERE code IN ('" + sCode + "', 'Z')");
	:RETURN aRows;
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE Lookup;
	:PARAMETERS sName, sCode;
	:DECLARE aRows;

	aRows := SQLExecute("SELECT custid FROM customers WHERE custname = '" + sName + "'");
	aRows := SQLExecute("SELECT id FROM c WHERE code IN ('" + sCode + "', 'Z')");

	:RETURN aRows;
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
4:72-4:73 warning sql_injection: String concatenation in 'SQLExecute' argument may cause SQL injection. Use parameterized queries instead.
5:58-5:59 warning sql_injection: String concatenation in 'SQLExecute' argument may cause SQL injection. Use parameterized queries instead.
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
4:22-4:71 info sql_literal_splice: SQL character literal spliced across string concatenation - prefer a ?param? placeholder: splices are fragile to format, invisible to the parameter APIs, and the classic injection surface
4:72-4:73 warning sql_injection: String concatenation in 'SQLExecute' argument may cause SQL injection. Use parameterized queries instead.
5:22-5:57 info sql_literal_splice: SQL character literal spliced across string concatenation - prefer a ?param? placeholder: splices are fragile to format, invisible to the parameter APIs, and the classic injection surface
5:58-5:59 warning sql_injection: String concatenation in 'SQLExecute' argument may cause SQL injection. Use parameterized queries instead.
